general_notes.general_notes:
This study uses normative modeling to derive individualized gray matter morphological abnormalities (Z-scores) which then serve as features for subtype identification rather than as clinical outcome measures themselves. The normative model training and validation procedures are not extensively detailed. The study emphasizes reproducibility and generalizability of subtyping results across different clustering algorithms (k-means, HYDRA), independent datasets, and unseen disorders. An additional dataset (SALD, n=492 healthy individuals aged 19-80) was used solely to construct the normal structural covariance network for disease epicenter mapping. The structural covariance network was constructed by computing pairwise correlation coefficients of regional gray matter volumes across subjects (246×246 matrix, negative correlations set to zero). Neurotransmitter receptor/transporter data were obtained from PET-derived atlas (Hansen and Shafiei, 2022b) covering 19 receptor types across multiple neurotransmitter systems (serotonin, norepinephrine, cannabinoid, dopamine, GABA, histamine, glutamate, opioid). Transcriptional data for 14 inflammation-related genes were obtained from Allen Human Brain Atlas (Hawrylycz et al., 2012b) and processed using AHBA pipeline with averaging across six donors. The study received ethical approval from the First Affiliated Hospital of Zhengzhou University (2018-KY-88). Detailed demographic information shows discovery dataset had significant group differences in age (p=0.026), sex (p=0.041), and education (p<0.001). Validation datasets showed no significant age or sex differences between patients and controls. Substance use history was high in discovery dataset (HCs 37%, Sch 70%, BD 85%, ADHD 59%).


